WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
These two GFP-filled vesicles are 150 nanometres apart.

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.160
An ordinary light microscope records one spot.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.020
Why can software zoom not split it back into two?

4
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.540
Follow the light from specimen to screen, and watch where the missing detail is

5
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.540
lost.

6
00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:23.957
Start with one point source.

7
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:28.897
In ideal widefield fluorescence, every point becomes a blur.

8
00:00:30.257 --> 00:00:33.737
The screen shows a bright centre with faint rings, an Airy pattern.

9
00:00:35.037 --> 00:00:38.717
This computed green curve is its intensity across the screen.

10
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.220
Why does the point spread?

11
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.520
Light diffracts at the objective opening.

12
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.540
A finer pattern bends light through wider angles.

13
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.840
The lens collects only a cone, so some information from fine detail misses the

14
00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.460
objective. Numerical aperture describes the width of the collected cone.

15
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:07.873
In this ideal lateral model, the Rayleigh distance equals 0.61 times emission

16
00:01:07.873 --> 00:01:10.713
wavelength, divided by numerical aperture.

17
00:01:12.153 --> 00:01:15.713
Emission wavelength is the colour released by the fluorescent label.

18
00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:21.533
In the ideal lateral model, the Rayleigh distance gets smaller with shorter emission

19
00:01:21.913 --> 00:01:24.293
wavelength or higher numerical aperture.

20
00:01:24.293 --> 00:01:29.493
It is one resolution convention, not a universal border.

21
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:32.377
Now restore both vesicles.

22
00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:36.397
Their separation is 150 nanometres.

23
00:01:37.857 --> 00:01:42.737
For the settings shown here, the displayed Rayleigh distance is about 227

24
00:01:42.737 --> 00:01:47.137
nanometres. Will the detector show two peaks, or one?

25
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:54.627
Each source still makes its own blur.

26
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:58.967
On the detector, their intensities add point by point.

27
00:02:00.147 --> 00:02:03.007
The two computed curves merge into one summed peak.

28
00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:07.947
At one hundred fifty nanometres, no dip survives between them.

29
00:02:09.127 --> 00:02:12.747
They are unresolved here, although other methods may separate them.

30
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:15.257
Move the sources apart.

31
00:02:16.557 --> 00:02:19.437
A shallow dip opens before they reach the Rayleigh distance.

32
00:02:20.697 --> 00:02:24.717
At that distance, the central dip is about one quarter of peak height.

33
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:29.517
That clear dip is the convention for calling two points resolved.

34
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:35.677
For two ideal points, the Rayleigh distance marks a clear dip, not the first

35
00:02:35.677 --> 00:02:36.777
appearance of two peaks.

36
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.180
Return the vesicles to 150 nanometres.

37
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.700
The detector again records one peak.

38
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:47.920
Software zoom enlarges that record.

39
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:52.580
It cannot add detail the optics and camera never captured.

40
00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.780
Optical magnification before the camera can change sampling; that is a different

41
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:03.840
operation. Bigger display pixels cannot recover a missing dip.

42
00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:10.123
To separate or interpret closer objects, change how information enters the record.

43
00:03:11.403 --> 00:03:15.663
A confocal pinhole asks which out-of-focus light reaches the detector.

44
00:03:17.123 --> 00:03:22.303
Super-resolution asks how illumination or glowing molecules can encode closer

45
00:03:22.303 --> 00:03:27.643
detail. Electron microscopy replaces light with a different probe.

46
00:03:28.423 --> 00:03:32.463
Each change answers a measurement problem and brings new limits.

47
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:37.250
A microscope image is a measurement, not the specimen itself.

48
00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:43.510
Before trusting one, ask what was labelled, what the instrument could separate, and

49
00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:44.690
what the detector recorded.

50
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.390
Software zoom makes the same record bigger.

51
00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:53.810
Software zoom cannot add detail the optics and camera never captured.
